---
import Callout from "../../../guide/Callout.astro";
import { sdk } from "../../../guide/extensions/sdk.ts";
import GuidePage from "../../../guide/GuidePage.astro";
import Path from "../../../guide/Path.astro";
import Snippet from "../../../guide/Snippet.astro";
import Task from "../../../guide/Task.astro";
import { sitePath } from "../../../site/links.ts";

const key = `
keytool -genkeypair -keystore extensions.jks -alias extensions -keyalg RSA -keysize 4096 -validity 36500
`;

const sign = `
./gradlew assembleRelease
zipalign -f 4 build/outputs/apk/release/example-comics-release-unsigned.apk example-comics.apk
apksigner sign --ks extensions.jks --ks-key-alias extensions --v4-signing-enabled false example-comics.apk
apksigner verify --print-certs example-comics.apk
`;

const fingerprint = `
Signer #1 certificate SHA-256 digest: 0ea9de3d5a682608a15b101000c032d5aaae7e12902fc6bf8bb551de1aa00124
`;

const index = `
{
  "name": "Example Extensions",
  "badgeLabel": "Example",
  "signingKey": "0ea9de3d5a682608a15b101000c032d5aaae7e12902fc6bf8bb551de1aa00124",
  "contact": { "website": "https://example.org" },
  "extensionList": {
    "extensions": [
      {
        "name": "Example Comics",
        "packageName": "org.example.katari.examplecomics",
        "resources": {
          "apkUrl": "https://example.org/katari/apk/example-comics.apk",
          "iconUrl": "https://example.org/katari/icon/example-comics.png"
        },
        "extensionLib": "${sdk.family}",
        "versionCode": 1,
        "versionName": "${sdk.family}.0",
        "contentWarning": "SAFE",
        "sources": [
          {
            "id": 4316702938517726312,
            "name": "Example Comics",
            "language": "en",
            "homeUrl": "https://comics.example",
            "supportedEntryTypes": ["MANGA"]
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
}
`;
---

<GuidePage
  slug="extensions/store"
  lead="An extension store is one JSON file, and the APKs and icons it points to, on any web server. Users add its address once and get every update from it."
>
  <Task id="sign" title="Sign every release with one key">
    <p>Make a key once, and keep it somewhere safe:</p>
    <Snippet lang="shell" code={key} />
    <p>Then build each release, align it, sign it, and read the key's fingerprint:</p>
    <Snippet lang="shell" code={sign} />
    <p>
      <code>zipalign</code> and <code>apksigner</code> are in the Android SDK's <code>build-tools</code>. Of what the last command prints,
      the store needs the SHA-256 digest, in the same form: 64 lowercase hexadecimal digits without colons. <code>keytool</code> prints
      it in capitals with colons, which matches nothing.
    </p>
    <Snippet lang="text" code={fingerprint} />
    <p>
      Katari installs an extension from a store only if the APK is signed with the store's key, and trusts it without asking for the same
      reason.
    </p>
    <Callout label="Keep the key" tone="warning">
      <p>
        Android installs an update only when it is signed with the same key as the installed app. If the key is lost, no extension it signed
        can be updated again. You would have to uninstall each one and install it again from a store with the new key.
      </p>
    </Callout>
  </Task>

  <Task id="index" title="The index">
    <p>The index describes the store and lists its extensions:</p>
    <Snippet file="index.json" lang="json" code={index} />
    <table class="terms">
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Field</th> <th>What it holds</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>name</code>, <code>badgeLabel</code></td> <td>Required. The store's name, and the short label shown on each of its extensions.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>signingKey</code></td> <td>Required. The SHA-256 digest of the signing certificate, as <code>apksigner</code> prints it.</td></tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>contact.website</code>, <code>contact.discord</code></td>
          <td>Where users find out about the store. The website is required, the Discord invite optional.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>extensionList.extensions</code></td>
          <td>The extensions. Leave <code>extensionList</code> out and give <code>extensionListUrl</code> instead to keep the list in a file of its own.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>Every field of an extension is required:</p>
    <table class="terms">
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Field</th> <th>What it holds</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>name</code>, <code>packageName</code></td> <td>The extension's name and its <code>applicationId</code>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>resources.apkUrl</code>, <code>resources.iconUrl</code></td> <td>Full addresses of the APK and its icon, which is a square PNG.</td></tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>extensionLib</code></td>
          <td>
            The SDK the extension is built with, the first two parts of its <code>versionName</code>, such as
            <code>"{sdk.family}"</code>. Katari hides extensions built with an SDK it doesn't load, such as SDK 2.x, and a value that
            isn't numbers, such as <code>"3.x"</code>, fails the whole store's list.
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>versionCode</code>, <code>versionName</code></td>
          <td>The same as in the APK. Katari offers an update when <code>versionCode</code> grows, or the SDK in <code>versionName</code> is newer.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr><td><code>contentWarning</code></td> <td><code>SAFE</code>, <code>MIXED</code> or <code>NSFW</code>, matching the manifest.</td></tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>sources</code></td>
          <td>
            At least one. Each source's <code>id</code>, <code>name</code> and <code>language</code>, required, and
            <code>homeUrl</code>, optional, as the source itself has them. Katari lists each source on its own before the extension is installed, in the languages the user
            turned on, and uses them to name a source a backup refers to and offer its extension.
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>supportedEntryTypes</code></td>
          <td>
            Optional, on each source: <code>MANGA</code>, <code>ANIME</code> or <code>BOOK</code>, for the type badges and the
            <strong>Content types</strong> filter. Leave it out and the source shows only when that filter includes unspecified sources.
          </td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      The index and a separate extension list can be served gzipped, and as Protobuf instead of JSON. Katari reads Tachiyomi's and
      Aniyomi's <code>index.min.json</code> stores and Aniyomi's newer index as well, but new stores should use this format.
    </p>
    <Callout label="Every listing needs its sources" tone="warning">
      <p>
        Katari's index and Aniyomi's newer one don't say which they are, so Katari reads the index both ways and keeps the reading in
        which more extensions fit: with at least one source and an <code>extensionLib</code> such as <code>"{sdk.family}"</code>. A
        listing that doesn't fit still shows, but an index in which none fits fails to load.
      </p>
    </Callout>
  </Task>

  <Task id="host" title="Put it online">
    <ol>
      <li>Upload <code>index.json</code>, the APKs and the icons to any server that serves files over HTTPS, such as GitHub Pages.</li>
      <li>
        Tell users the address of <code>index.json</code>. They paste it under <Path>Browse › Extensions › ⋮ › Extension stores</Path>,
        as in <a href={sitePath("guide/first-steps/#extension-store")}>First steps</a>.
      </li>
      <li>
        For a one-tap button on a web page, link to <code>katari://extension-store?url=</code> followed by the index's address, encoded as
        a URL parameter. Katari opens and asks the user to confirm.
      </li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      To release an update, upload the new APK, raise its <code>versionCode</code> and <code>versionName</code> in the index, and Katari
      offers it the next time it checks the store. An installed extension the index no longer lists keeps working, marked
      <strong>Orphaned</strong>.
    </p>
  </Task>
</GuidePage>
